# The three test tiers, in one place, because two copies drift.
#
# Read by the Makefile (`include tests/tiers.env`) and by
# .github/workflows/ci.yml, which greps the assignments into $GITHUB_ENV.
# Nothing else may spell a tier filter; a guard test asserts both readers derive
# from this file, and that neither contains a hand-written one.
#
# NOT sourceable shell, despite looking like it (#490). `. tests/tiers.env`
# dies on the unquoted `&` in the filters, and AUTONATE_TIER_SERVICES would try
# to run `Dapr` as a command. Both real readers are fine -- make's `include` and
# a grep are not shell evaluation -- but do not write `source tests/tiers.env`
# in a script and expect it to work.
#
#   slim          no RequiresService trait          what GitHub runs
#   full-local    everything except Keycloak        a developer's machine
#   full-keycloak everything                        future
#
# VSTest has no trait-ABSENCE operator, so "no trait" is the negated conjunction
# of every known service. That is why AUTONATE_TIER_SERVICES exists beside the
# filters rather than the filters being written out by hand: a service added to
# the list without being added to the slim filter would silently move its tests
# into slim, and a guard test derives one from the other.

# Every service a RequiresService trait may name. A trait carrying anything else
# fails a guard, because a typo would otherwise land its test in slim.
AUTONATE_TIER_SERVICES=Flowable Dapr Keycloak

# Derived from the line above. Kept literal rather than computed because
# `dotnet test --filter` is passed through several layers of quoting and a
# computed string is one shell away from being wrong in a way nothing notices.
AUTONATE_TIER_SLIM_FILTER=RequiresService!=Flowable&RequiresService!=Dapr&RequiresService!=Keycloak
AUTONATE_TIER_FULL_LOCAL_FILTER=RequiresService!=Keycloak
AUTONATE_TIER_FULL_KEYCLOAK_FILTER=

# --- Size pins (#453) ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# What each filter must discover in tests/AutoNate.E2E.Tests. Checked by
# infra/tier-integrity.sh at the end of `make test-full-local`, because a tier
# that silently gets smaller reports success for the wrong reason.
#
# EXACT, not floors. House style (ExecutionOracleSizeTests' 29, the coverage
# ratchet): growth has to be as visible as loss, or the number drifts upward
# and stops meaning anything. When you add tests, move the pin in the same
# commit -- that is the point, not the friction.
#
# Per service as well as per tier, because the tier total cannot see a test
# re-traited OUT of Flowable: it stays in full-local, it just stops needing the
# engine. That is exactly how a live-engine oracle gets quietly defanged.
#
# Measured 2026-09-15 at ddb58e9: 172 + 198 + 1 = 371, + 5 Keycloak = 376.
# full-local moved to 386 the same day by #474, which added 15 untraited
# BpmnDiagramHelperTests. The service pins stayed put -- that is the shape of a
# healthy growth: tests joined slim, nothing left Flowable.
# Then 203/403 by #471, which proved the three activity-marker rows: +3 cells
# and +2 negative controls under Flowable, +26 untraited diagram-helper tests.
# Then 208/414 by #522, the oracle-instrument story: the effect vocabulary gained
# `instance-starts` and `host-cancelled`, and each new name arrives with BOTH a
# negative control and -- because no manifest row declares it yet -- a positive
# one, so +4 under Flowable. The 3 untraited AttachedHostOf cases joined slim
# (202 -> 205), and the backend gained the reason floor and the two pairing
# guards (2729 -> 2732). No row declares either new effect here; that is the
# eleven coverage stories' work, one ratchet step each.
# The first slice of M4d landed at 223/440/216/2733. Then #523 added the signal
# broadcast route: +4 Flowable (the live wake, fan-out, refusal and scope
# complement) and +11 slim backend (6 broadcaster unit tests that need no engine,
# 5 gate tests). The split is deliberate -- the decision rules are unit-testable
# and belong where a merge can see them; only "a broadcast moves instances" needs
# Flowable.
# Then 210/416 by #526, which proved the two conditional rows. #525 moved nothing
# because it retired #522's two positive controls as it declared the two effects
# they stood in for; there was nothing left to retire here, so these are +2.
# The notes above are per-STORY and were written as each landed, which is why
# "Then 210/416 by #526" sits BELOW "The first slice of M4d landed at
# 223/440/216/2733" and reads backwards. They then stopped entirely, while the
# pins kept moving (#550). What actually landed, by merge, as
# Flowable / full-local / slim-e2e / slim-backend:
#
#   #538  first slice   c3be2b1   223 / 440 / 216 / 2733
#   #539  second slice  b3e2845   230 / 447 / 216 / 2744
#   #542  final slice   034630c   234 / 451 / 216 / 2763
#   #543                ddb75dd   235 / 452 / 216 / 2764
#
# The per-story notes explain the why of each step; this is the ledger they were
# being checked against, and a pin whose history is unwritten is a number nobody
# can audit -- which is the same failure the pins themselves exist to prevent.
#
# M4d's fix pass (#544, #546-#550) moved four of the seven. FLOWABLE 235 -> 238:
# the two row-level negative controls #546 was about (escalation and conditional
# boundaries, non-interrupting) and #544's live proof that an unpublished draft
# declares nothing. SLIM_E2E 216 -> 223: #549's five RoutesByScript cases and
# #548's two paging cases -- the latter deliberately in the E2E project but
# UNTRAITED, because the helper it drives lives there and the tier that blocks a
# merge is where a paging regression has to be visible. SLIM_BACKEND 2764 ->
# 2771: #549's two snapshot-site prefix cases plus its theory's second row,
# #547's two dispatcher warnings and #544's two broadcaster draft cases.
# Round two of the M4d fix pass (#552-#555) moved three. SLIM_BACKEND 2771 ->
# 2785: #552's store guard for the published-version join, #553's three registry
# guards, and #554's five prefix-site theories (10 rows -- every one of #482's
# seven sites is now reached, which took two goes because both #549 and its own
# fix miscounted in the same direction). SLIM_E2E 223 -> 224 and FULL_LOCAL
# 462 -> 463: #555's throwing-page case. FLOWABLE is unchanged at 238 -- this
# round added no live-engine test, which is itself the shape to notice: the
# blocker it closed was a missing guard, not a missing proof.
# Round three (#557-#559) moved one pin: SLIM_BACKEND 2785 -> 2792. Seven facts,
# all guards for code that already shipped correct -- #557's two on the published
# lookup and two on the send behaviour, #558's two on the legacy-script scan and
# one on the post-publish rename. The E2E pins did not move, and FLOWABLE has now
# been 238 for three rounds: nothing since the milestone's last slice has needed
# a live engine, because every round since has been about evidence rather than
# behaviour.
# Round four (#561-#563): SLIM_BACKEND 2792 -> 2794. Net of three added and one
# DELETED -- #561's two guards on the key-change and the reused-key ordering,
# #562's one covering both published queries' join, and the removal of a
# broadcaster test that four rounds of sharpening never made non-redundant with
# one shipped in #539. A pin going up by two while three tests were written is
# the arithmetic working: growth and loss are both visible, which is the whole
# reason the pins are exact.
# M5's first story, #574 (the wildcard's own compiled form): SLIM_BACKEND
# 2794 -> 2798. Four added facts: the deny direction (a wildcard deny must
# WITHHOLD the rows that have a value), the array tag's non-empty meaning, the
# execution compiler's own widening, and the compiler-scope guard that pins the
# wildcard branch to exactly the two cache compilers.
#
# A fifth change moved no count. `The_wildcard_divergence_still_holds` was
# RENAMED to `The_wildcard_agrees_and_means_has_any_value` and its assertion
# inverted, rather than deleted -- so the wildcard's semantics are still
# asserted in one named place, and the rename is visible in the diff where a
# delete-plus-add would not have been. The same is true of
# SelectorGeneratorCoverageTests' `Assert.DoesNotContain` -> `Assert.Contains`:
# an inverted assertion inside an existing fact, so +0.
#
# FLOWABLE and SLIM_E2E unchanged -- none of it needs an engine, and the
# identity still holds: 224 + 238 + 4 - 3 = 463.
#
# #111 (a business rule task runs a decision table): 254 -> 258, and FULL_LOCAL
# 489 -> 493. Four things, all needing the engine:
#   +1  the live-engine oracle's `Business Rule Task` cell. ONE theory row, not a
#       new class -- so the count moves in three places that have to agree: here,
#       `ExecutionOracleSizeTests.Cells` (slim, where CI can see it) and the
#       `obliged` list in `ExecutionEvidenceTests`.
#   +3  version binding (both directions), a deleted table refused at publish, and
#       a failed evaluation surfacing as a job rather than a 500 on start.
# SLIM_E2E is unchanged: every one of them is `RequiresService=Flowable`, because
# it is the engine that decides and nothing about that is provable without one.
# The slim half of the same work is 10 backend facts (2913 -> 2923).
# Identity: 234 + 258 + 4 - 3 = 493.
#
# #173 (a multi-instance activity as one row with progress): FLOWABLE 261 -> 264,
# FULL_LOCAL 495 -> 498, SLIM_BACKEND 2932 -> 2946. SLIM_E2E unchanged at 233.
#
# The backend's +14 is two commits' worth, and saying so is the point of these
# notes: +7 landed with the collapse itself (5 progress facts, 2 endpoint facts)
# and +7 with the round that followed -- 3 pinning that ONE recorded failure
# across three instances counts as one rather than three, and 4 covering the
# engine-sourced total and the per-instance collection item.
#
# The +3 under Flowable is the split worth reading. Everything decidable from XML
# or from a stubbed engine went to slim, where a merge can see it; what needs a
# live engine is exactly what was MEASURED rather than reasoned: that a parallel
# loop's three rows collapse to one, that a sequential loop in flight writes ONE
# historic row for a loop of three (so the total must come from the engine's
# `nrOfInstances`, not from counting rows), and that five instances are still one
# row on the executions list. None of those three can be proved without Flowable,
# and the middle one is the reason the story's design changed.
# #231 (an accumulating complex-gateway join): FLOWABLE 261 -> 264, FULL_LOCAL
# 495 -> 498, SLIM_BACKEND 2932 -> 2937. SLIM_E2E unchanged at 233.
#
# The +3 under Flowable are the three claims only a live engine can settle, and
# they are claims about ACCUMULATION OVER TIME rather than about one token: that
# branches arriving OUT OF ORDER fire the join exactly once (measured on the
# engine as arrived='in2,in1', fired=True, route='fj'), that a later arrival on a
# fired join is absorbed without the routing script being consulted again, and
# that a withheld branch leaves the join waiting -- the owner's no-timeout
# decision, asserted rather than assumed.
#
# The +5 in slim are the expansion's shape, which is a pure function over XML:
# one accumulator per incoming flow, the single-inbound complement that pins
# #218's shape as UNCHANGED, the rewiring, the absorbing end event and the wait
# route joining the allowed-route contract.
#
# One of those five earned its place the hard way. The wait flow's condition was
# built before the flow was attached, so its xsi:type came out as a bare
# `tFormalExpression` and Flowable refused every join publish with
# `cvc-elt.4.2`. The unit test asserted the condition's VALUE, which is identical
# either way, and only the engine tier caught it. It now asserts the prefix too,
# which is a publish-breaking bug moved down to the merge gate where it belongs.
#
# BOTH of the notes above are true, and the pins are their SUM rather than either
# one: #173 and #231 were built on separate branches off the same master, so each
# note records the move it measured from 261/495/2932. Landed together they are
# +3 and +3 under Flowable and +14 and +5 in slim.
#
# #78 (studio property-editor coverage): FLOWABLE 267 -> 268, SLIM_E2E 233 -> 240,
# FULL_LOCAL 501 -> 509. SLIM_BACKEND unchanged -- this story writes no backend
# test.
#
# The split is the story's own instruction and it is worth reading. SEVEN of the
# eight are UNTRAITED: the `data-element-id` guard and the six editor journeys
# open a diagram, edit it and reload it, and none of them publishes, so none needs
# Flowable. `WorkflowStudioTests` carries the trait per CLASS, so putting canvas
# coverage there would have pushed it into a tier no pull request runs -- which is
# exactly how #356 stayed broken for four rounds.
#
# The ONE under Flowable is the half that genuinely needs an engine: publishing
# three workflows, starting three instances, and opening the task each completion
# mode produces.
#
# One of the seven is INVERTED and pins a defect (#624): the studio silently
# discards a user task's form mode on save. It is counted here like any other
# test because it runs and must keep running -- when #624 is fixed it goes red,
# which is the point, and the pin does not move because flipping an assertion
# adds no test.
#
# The M5 verification fix pass: SLIM_BACKEND 2951 -> 2959. FLOWABLE, SLIM_E2E and
# FULL_LOCAL unchanged -- every one of the eight is a slim backend test, which is
# itself the point. These are guards, and a guard that only runs full-local is
# how six of the ten defects survived in the first place.
#
# The eight, by the bug they close:
#   #626  1  the recorded failure carries no engine internals to /history
#   #634  2  a finished run is served, not deleted -- plus the complement that an
#            ACTIVE run the engine no longer lists still IS deleted
#   #630  1  order=desc actually reaches the history URL
#   #629  1  a late poll page cannot un-complete a swept task
#   #627  1  a cache miss still collapses, because the read-through answers
#   #632  2  a deny naming a withdrawn tag refuses -- plus the complement that a
#            deny naming a LIVE tag that does not match still allows
#
# #633 and #635 moved no pin: one replaced a dead assertion inside an existing
# fact, the other widened a scan and INVERTED a test that was asserting something
# false. Neither adds a test, and both change what the suite can see -- which is
# the shape of a fix that a count cannot show.
#
# Identity: 240 + 268 + 4 - 3 = 509.
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_FLOWABLE=294
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_DAPR=4
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_KEYCLOAK=5

# #524 took Dapr from 1 to 3, and that is the pin doing something new. Until then
# the Dapr trait covered one page-load test; the queue-start pair is the first
# thing in the suite that actually PUBLISHES to the bus and asserts the app acted
# on it. Both carry Flowable too, because starting a workflow needs an engine --
# so the same two tests move both service pins, which is correct rather than
# double counting: each pin asks "how many tests need THIS service".
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_FULL_LOCAL=540

# Tests needing MORE THAN ONE service, counted once here so the identity
# SLIM_E2E + FLOWABLE + DAPR - MULTI_SERVICE = FULL_LOCAL stays exact (#524).
#
# The identity assumed every E2E test needs at most one service, which was true
# until one needed two: the queue-start pair publishes to the bus AND starts a
# workflow, so it carries both traits, lands in both service pins, and lands once
# in full-local. Each service pin still answers exactly the question it should --
# "how many tests need THIS service" -- and the arithmetic learns about the
# overlap rather than the traits being bent to keep the arithmetic simple.
#
# PINNED, not computed. A term derived from the other pins would absorb the drift
# the identity exists to catch: FLOWABLE=150 would pass again by calling the
# missing 84 an overlap. `The_multi_service_pin_matches_the_classes_that_carry_two_traits`
# counts it from the source.
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_MULTI_SERVICE=3

#
# #575 (path ids and nested predicates on the SQL path): 2798 -> 2803. Five
# added facts -- the path-id filter and its complement, the empty-path-id case,
# the execution compiler's own path filter, the nested subject set, and the
# nesting neither path resolves. No fact was removed or renamed.
#
# The agreement property itself did not move the count and is where most of the
# evidence is: lifting the generator's path-id and nesting exclusions took it
# from clean to 2560 leaks and 225 lockouts against the pre-fix compilers.
#
# #576 (status supplied in memory, tenant withdrawn): 2803 -> 2807. Four facts --
# the list-endpoint builder supplies a NORMALIZED status, the instance
# authorizer derives one from the suspension flag, a `[status=...]` grant selects
# and REFUSES the same rows on both paths, and `tenant` is neither advertised nor
# compiled.
#
# #577 (an uncompilable deny fails closed): 2807 -> 2810. Three facts, one per
# direction plus the boundary: a deny that will not compile closes the request,
# an allow that will not compile is still skipped while the OTHER allows still
# grant, and an uncompilable allow on its own grants nothing rather than
# everything. The middle one is the complement that matters -- without it, a
# change that failed EVERY uncompilable grant closed would pass the first fact
# while turning an allow typo into an outage.
#
# #583 (the execution cache learns the run's name): 2810 -> 2815. Five facts --
# a projected row carries both names, a null name is distinguishable from a row
# the previous projection version wrote, a re-poll fills one in, an empty name
# normalizes to null so the UI's `name ?? id` fallback fires, and the
# read-through write path carries the name WITHOUT blanking the model name the
# poll wrote. That last one is the only test that fails when the read-through
# regresses, and it is the regression that was live until this story.
#
# #579 (a single execution is authorized from the cache): 2815 -> 2819. Four
# facts -- the authorizer and the list path build identical facts; a permitted
# actor is authorized with the engine unreachable; an unpermitted one is still
# refused; and a cache miss with the engine unreachable refuses rather than
# admitting on absent evidence.
#
# ExecutionStatusFactTests moved no count: #576's suspension-inference fact was
# REWRITTEN into a cache-row fact, because #579 gave that path a real status
# column and the two-state ceiling the inference had is gone.
#
# #586 (the task cache learns a task finished): 2819 -> 2823. Four facts -- a
# finished task is marked completed; a sweep that fails midway leaves open tasks
# open; a second sweep stops on a FULL page that marks nothing; and
# FlowsQueryEntity's open-task filter now excludes a row it previously could not.
#
# #588 (page the execution fetch): 2823 -> 2828. Five facts -- it pages past the
# first full page; an exactly-full page is not assumed to be the end; a short page
# ends that collection without erroring; maxPages bounds the work on a large
# backlog; and a failed page THROWS rather than returning a short list.
#
# The middle two are one complement in both directions. Getting either backwards
# produces a plausible-looking wrong answer: treat a full page as the end and the
# list silently truncates at exactly one page; treat a short page as a failure and
# every partial collection looks like an outage.
#
# #104 (execution reads move to the cache): 2828 -> 2834. Six facts -- four for
# /{id}/tasks served from workflow_task_cache (open tasks with the instance name;
# a COMPLETED task absent; still serving with the engine unreachable; 404 for an
# unknown instance) and two for the live-read guard, which names every GET route
# still permitted to inject IFlowableClient and asserts the set in BOTH
# directions, so a route cannot quietly rejoin it.
#
# #108 (the executions list becomes a SQL query): 2834 -> 2840. Six facts -- more
# than 200 visible executions are returned; paging is over the AUTHORIZED set; an
# execution the actor may not see is absent; the API's status vocabulary is
# preserved; the Errored overlay keeps the live path's precedence (Cancelled beats
# Errored); and "last activity" comes from the event log rather than falling back
# to the start time.
#
# #594 (the executions view says how current it is): 2840 -> 2845. Five facts --
# a quiet system reports UPDATING; a stalled feed reports NOT updating while its
# rows still look fine; a feed that never completed a sweep reports not updating;
# asOf is the OLDEST sync among visible rows, not the newest; and the endpoint
# carries an authorization decision.
#
# The first two are one complement in both directions. Either alone is satisfiable
# by a stub: report "updating" always and the quiet-system test passes; report
# "stalled" always and the stalled-feed test passes.
#
# #109 (execution staleness made visible): SLIM_E2E 224 -> 228, and FULL_LOCAL
# 463 -> 467 with it. FOUR untraited Playwright facts -- the view states how
# current it is; the indicator is a POLITE live region with role=status; and the
# refresh control is reachable by keyboard alone (walked with Tab, not focused
# directly) and operable with Enter; plus one added after CI went red, which
# forces the stopped-feed state by deleting the feed's watermark and asserts the
# indicator does NOT wear role="alert".
#
# That fourth one exists because the third failure was invisible locally. The dev
# app had polled, so the indicator always read fresh here and the branch never
# rendered; CI has no heartbeat, so it did. Forcing the state makes the condition
# reproducible in both places rather than only where it happened to occur.
#
# They are E2E rather than unit tests because they are DOM claims and the SPA's
# vitest runs with `environment: "node"`. The decision logic those assertions sit
# on top of is unit-tested in src/lib/__tests__/execution-freshness.test.ts, where
# it can be. SLIM_BACKEND does not move: this story adds no xUnit facts.
#
# #590 (the history feed's watermark does nothing): 2845 -> 2849. Four facts --
# a second tick over unchanged history fetches strictly less; a tick with nothing
# new fetches ONE page; a newer event is still read; and an event sharing the
# watermark's exact timestamp is NOT dropped.
#
# The last was added after a mutation survived. Changing the stop from "strictly
# older" to "older or equal" passed all three original facts, because none of them
# could see an event being silently lost at the boundary.
#
# #581 (candidateuser/candidategroup withdrawn): 2849 -> 2848. A DECREASE, and
# the house style is that loss is as visible as growth.
#
# `An_array_tags_wildcard_matches_a_non_empty_array` (#574) is removed because its
# subject is gone: CompileArrayContains existed only for those two tags, and both
# are withdrawn. `The_known_candidate_tag_divergence_still_holds` was INVERTED
# rather than deleted -- it pinned a divergence that no longer exists -- so it
# moves no count.
#
# The tags were not merely divergent, they were backed by nothing:
# FlowableTaskProjection.MapRow writes Array.Empty<string>() for both columns
# unconditionally. Measured: 8,040 task rows, zero with a candidate list.
#
# #585 (an unloadable plugin is a 400, not a 500): 2848 -> 2851. Three facts --
# two Theory rows for two distinct corrupt-assembly shapes, and the complement
# that a genuinely loadable plugin still enables.
#
# Two rows rather than one on purpose: the loader's exception type for corrupt
# input is not contractual, so a mapping tuned to the one byte sequence anyone
# happened to try is a mapping that will be wrong again.
#
# --- Slim's own pins (#476) ---------------------------------------------------
#
# The tier that actually blocks merges had neither a skip check nor a count
# floor, while full had both. Checked inside GitHub's gate, not only in
# `make test-slim`: a developer's machine is not the thing that blocks a merge.
#
# Per project, because they fail differently. The backend shards run UNFILTERED
# (ci.yml applies the slim filter to the E2E project only, and no backend test
# carries a RequiresService trait), so its pin catches deletion. The E2E pin
# also catches a trait being added to a slim class: the filter stops matching
# it, the count drops, and the gate is red until the pin moves in the same
# commit -- which is the whole point, since that shrink used to be invisible.
#
# NO COMMIT IS NAMED HERE, DELIBERATELY (#563).
#
# This line has carried a wrong sha three times running -- `d34829d` when the
# pins had moved three times since (#555), then `962be85` (#559) and `0b93270`
# (#563), each the commit BEFORE the values underneath it were measured. Three
# times is not carelessness, it is a mechanism: a commit cannot name its own
# hash, so whoever writes the line is always naming the parent, and the sentence
# reads as checked while being wrong by construction.
#
# The provenance is recoverable without being written down, and recovering it
# beats trusting it:
#
# M5's refusal-and-refresh trio (#578, #234, #268) moved all four. SLIM_BACKEND
# 2851 -> 2856: #578's three multi-pool facts and #234's two on the prepared
# flag. FLOWABLE 238 -> 240: #578's live-engine refusal and #268's Page-mode
# task. SLIM_E2E 228 -> 229: #234's save-versus-publish spec, which is refused
# in the browser before any request leaves it, so it needs no engine. FULL_LOCAL
# 467 -> 470, with the identity 229 + 240 + 4 - 3 = 470 holding -- each number
# read off a real discovery run, not arithmetic over the others.
# #106 (the DMN engine): SLIM_BACKEND 2856 -> 2864, FLOWABLE 240 -> 243,
# FULL_LOCAL 470 -> 473. SLIM_E2E does not move -- all three new E2E facts need
# the engine.
#
# The split is deliberate and is the same one PlacementDifferentialTests uses.
# The EIGHT slim facts pin what the client sends and how it reads the answer, so
# a regression fails the merge gate; the THREE full-local facts pin what the
# engine actually does, because only the engine can answer that.
#
# One of those engine facts exists to keep a client behaviour honest rather than
# to test the engine's happy path: a type-mismatched input is ACCEPTED, 201, with
# an empty result -- byte-identical to a legitimate no-match. The client refuses
# such a call itself, and that refusal is superstition unless something pins the
# behaviour it compensates for.
# #229 (an interrupting handler that does not interrupt): SLIM_BACKEND 2864 ->
# 2868, FLOWABLE 243 -> 245, FULL_LOCAL 473 -> 475.
#
# The two engine facts are a DIFFERENTIAL, and one shape would not have been a
# measurement: #162's first probe used only the arrangement that fails to
# interrupt, and stopping there would have produced "Flowable's interrupting
# event subprocess does not interrupt" -- false. A third arrangement, everything
# at the process level, turned out to interrupt correctly too, which is what made
# the warning's condition precise rather than approximate.
#
# Three of the four slim facts are NEGATIVE cases. A rule that warned about every
# event subprocess would satisfy the positive one and teach authors to ignore the
# warning.
# #286 (a compensation handler sees current values): FLOWABLE 245 -> 246 and
# FULL_LOCAL 475 -> 476. One fact, and it asserts the VALUE the handler read
# rather than that the handler ran -- the limitation was documented only in the
# decision ledger, so nothing would have noticed Flowable changing this in either
# direction. SLIM_BACKEND does not move: only a live engine can answer it.
# #172 (jobs and timers an operator can see and act on): all four move.
# SLIM_BACKEND 2868 -> 2870, FLOWABLE 246 -> 250, SLIM_E2E 229 -> 231,
# FULL_LOCAL 476 -> 482. Identity 231 + 250 + 4 - 3 = 482, from real discovery.
#
# The split is what the claims need, not a preference. The FOUR engine facts
# assert effects only an engine can produce: a retry that advances the process
# past the step that had exhausted its retries, and a timer that fires at the new
# time. Both are asserted on the engine's own HISTORY rather than the task list --
# measured during the story, the cached task list still showed the
# force-completed "Step one" while history already had the next activity open, so
# a task-list assertion would have been a measure of projection latency.
#
# The TWO slim E2E facts cover what needs no engine and most wants a merge gate:
# that an unreachable engine reads differently from an execution with no jobs
# (both render no rows, and only the words tell them apart), and that the failure
# detail expands by Tab and Enter alone.
#
# The TWO slim backend facts are the gate independence, one grant per theory,
# each checked against BOTH endpoints. Three of those four assertions would pass
# against a single shared gate.
# #110 (author a decision table in-app): all four move. SLIM_BACKEND 2870 ->
# 2906, FLOWABLE 250 -> 252, SLIM_E2E 231 -> 233, FULL_LOCAL 482 -> 486.
# Identity 233 + 252 + 4 - 3 = 486, from real discovery.
#
# THIRTY-SIX slim backend facts, and most of them are negative. The validator's
# eighteen include one that accepts every cell shape a real table is made of --
# an empty cell meaning "any", a dash, a range, a comparison, a comma list, a FEEL
# function call -- because a validator that refused everything would satisfy every
# "is rejected" test and be useless.
#
# The two FLOWABLE facts are the generator's real output, created and published
# through Auton8's own API rather than a hand-written copy of what it "should"
# produce. The first version of that test deployed such a copy and claimed in its
# own comment to be kept in step by failing when the two diverged -- which it could
# not do, because nothing in it called the generator.
#
# The two slim E2E facts are the grid, which the story names as the control type
# most often built mouse-only: a cell reached by pressing Tab until focus lands on
# it, never by FocusAsync, and a column added widening the rules that already
# exist.
# #111 (a business rule task runs a decision table): SLIM_BACKEND 2906 -> 2913,
# FLOWABLE 252 -> 254, SLIM_E2E 233 -> 234, FULL_LOCAL 486 -> 489.
# Identity 234 + 254 + 4 - 3 = 489, from real discovery.
#
# The SLIM_E2E fact is the studio round trip, and it is in this tier rather than
# FLOWABLE on purpose: bpmn-js silently drops what its moddle does not model, so
# whether an autonate: attribute survives a save is a property of the vendored
# BUNDLE and needs a browser, not an engine. It asserts against what /prepare
# carried -- bpmn-js's own serialisation -- and then polls the stored row.
#   CORRECTED BELOW: that argument is about what the test proves, not about what
#   its fixture needs, and the fixture publishes a decision table. See the #111
#   entry further down.
#
# The two FLOWABLE facts are the ones only an engine can answer: a gateway after
# the task takes the path the table chose (two runs, same definition, different
# amounts), and a task with no table is refused at publish without the engine's
# NoClassDefFoundError reaching the author.
#
# #111 again, finishing the story's remaining AC: SLIM_BACKEND 2913 -> 2923,
# FLOWABLE 255 -> 259, SLIM_E2E 234 -> 233, FULL_LOCAL 490 -> 493. Identity
# 233 + 259 + 4 - 3 = 493, from real discovery.
#
# SLIM_E2E goes DOWN by one, and that is a correction rather than a loss.
# `BusinessRuleTaskStudioTests` shipped untraited on the argument recorded above
# -- the round trip is a property of the vendored bpmn-js bundle and needs a
# browser, not an engine -- which is true of what it PROVES and false of what it
# needs to SET UP. The picker lists only published tables, and publishing one
# deploys DMN to Flowable. It passed on a machine where the engine happens to be
# running and failed `Connection refused (localhost:8080)` the first time CI ever
# executed it. The bundle property still has a slim guard on GitHub: the vitest
# case `business-rule-task-round-trip.test.js` drives bpmn-js directly.
#
# The ten slim facts are the halves a stub can answer: which key publish resolved,
# that it landed in the DEPLOYED copy and not the stored one, that an unresolved
# key is refused with NOTHING deployed, that a key the pin map does not name passes
# through untouched, that re-keying a published version changes its identity and
# not its rules, that the deployed task carries the async stamp, and that no key an
# author is allowed to write can collide with another table's pinned name. Two of
# them go through the real endpoint against a stubbed engine, which is what lets
# "refused at deployment rather than at execution" be checked on GitHub.
#
# The three FLOWABLE facts are again the ones only an engine can answer: that an
# already-deployed definition keeps deciding the old way while one deployed after
# a republish reads the new version (both directions -- either alone passes for a
# different bug), that a deleted table is refused before anything reaches the
# engine, and that a failing evaluation becomes a job carrying the engine's
# message instead of a 500 on start with no instance behind it.
# #604 (a task completion is visible to the caller that made it): SLIM_BACKEND
# 2923 -> 2925. Two facts, both on the merge gate GitHub runs, because the 24
# specs that actually prove this are full-local and GitHub never sees them --
# which is exactly how a minute-stale detail view shipped. One asserts the
# successor is listed and the finished task is not, in the SAME request that
# completed it; one asserts the engine's 404 on an already-finished task reaches
# the caller as 409 rather than 500.
#
# E2E counts are unchanged: this fixed 24 existing specs rather than adding any.
# #609 (the executions list reflects the write that caused it): 2925 -> 2929.
# The same defect as #604 on the sibling cache, and found by it -- these are the
# four full-local specs still failing after #604 took that tier from 24 to 4, all
# of them "start it, then find it in the list". Four slim facts, one per write
# the list has to reflect: a start projects the instance, a cancel marks the row
# cancelled rather than removing it, a delete removes it, and a projection that
# fails does not fail a start the engine already accepted.
#
# E2E counts are unchanged again: four existing specs fixed, none added.
# #608 (each test app gets its own plugin directory): 2929 -> 2930. One fact, and
# it FORCES the race rather than waiting for it: a second app is started between
# the upload and the enable, so its orphan sweep is guaranteed to run against a
# database that has never heard of the first app's plugin. The existing tests pass
# on a machine where the race does not land -- which is how this survived two
# green CI runs and was then misdiagnosed as a local rebuild.
# #222 (a synchronous step failure reaches the executions error surface):
# SLIM_BACKEND 2930 -> 2932, FLOWABLE 259 -> 261, FULL_LOCAL 493 -> 495.
# Identity: 233 + 261 + 4 - 3 = 495.
#
# The two FLOWABLE facts are the two halves the issue separates, and one of them
# started as a MEASUREMENT it asked for: whether an ASYNC failure records a row
# was "not established" and is now established -- it does, within seconds,
# carrying the root cause rather than the wrapper. Kept as a guard rather than
# deleted, because nothing else asserts end to end the path the surface was built
# for. The other drives a gateway condition that throws, which is the case that
# genuinely could not reach it.
#
# The two slim facts are the halves a stub can answer: the engine's own text does
# not reach the caller, and the row lands attributed to the activity the OPERATOR
# acted on. The second carries `activity 'split'` inside the stubbed engine
# message deliberately, so it fails the moment anyone starts parsing that message
# for an id an author controls.
#     git log -S 'AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_SLIM_BACKEND=2932' -- tests/tiers.env
#
# names the commit that set any pin, correctly, forever. The pins themselves are
# checked by infra/tier-integrity.sh on every full-local run, so a wrong number
# fails a build -- which is a stronger guarantee than a sha in a comment was ever
# going to be.
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_SLIM_BACKEND=3028
AUTONATE_TIER_COUNT_SLIM_E2E=245

# #628 (#173's keyboard and announcement AC, which had no executable coverage at
# any tier): FLOWABLE 268 -> 271, FULL_LOCAL 509 -> 512. SLIM_BACKEND and
# SLIM_E2E do not move, and that is the honest place for these three.
#
# They are browser facts about a row that does not exist without a real
# multi-instance execution behind it, so they need the engine. The wording and
# the arithmetic they depend on are already slim, in
# `multiInstanceProgress.test.ts`; what is added here is what only a browser
# answers -- tab order, the ARIA state, and the live region.

# #631 (selector tag values compared case-INsensitively in memory and
# case-sensitively in SQL): SLIM_BACKEND 2959 -> 2962. Three facts -- two that a
# grant authored in the wrong case now matches in a LIST, and one that `%` and
# `_` stay literal characters.
#
# The third is not symmetry. `EF.Functions.ILike` is the one-word way to make
# this change and it passes both of the others, so a revert-shaped mutation
# certifies neither it nor them: measured, reverting kills the two case facts and
# leaves the wildcard one green, while ILIKE kills the wildcard one and leaves
# the two case facts green. Two mutations, because one covers two thirds of what
# was written.
#
# No E2E pin moves. The agreement property already covered this shape across 200
# generated selectors -- it just could not build a case-varying literal, every
# value in its pool being lowercase already. With the variants added and the fix
# reverted it reports 1567 lockouts and 0 leaks; with the fix, none. That is a
# generator change, not a test-count change, which is exactly the kind of growth
# a count cannot show.

# #79 (the execution admin controls, E2E-060): FLOWABLE 271 -> 278, FULL_LOCAL
# 512 -> 519. Seven facts, one per operator control -- reassign, due date,
# force-complete, move-state, variables, history+log, bulk-delete -- each
# asserted on the effect read back from the engine or the engine-facing API,
# with its complement. Flowable-traited because the controls act on a running
# instance and a stub would only replay what the spec already believes. Neither
# slim pin moves.

# #169 (a collaboration publishes as one deployment holding a definition per
# pool): SLIM_BACKEND 2962 -> 2975, FLOWABLE 278 -> 280, FULL_LOCAL 519 -> 521.
#
# Backend +13: MultiPoolPublishRefusalTests grew from 3 facts to 12 -- #578's
# "two pools are refused" INVERTED to "two executable pools are no longer
# refused", three new refusals (dangling processRef, duplicate process id, no
# executable pool), the save-path facts the replan found uncovered (the
# participant follows the rename; the primary is the first pool WITH a flow, not
# the first in the file; a counterparty is marked non-executable), and what the
# studio is told. Plus WorkflowPublishCompensationTests' four: deploy succeeded,
# record failed, deployment withdrawn -- the atomicity claim a "deliberately
# invalid second pool" cannot make, because Flowable refuses a whole file at
# parse and a bad pool never deploys anything.
#
# Flowable +2: the E2E that proved the refusal now proves the capability, and
# gained "republishing advances the UNCHANGED pool" -- the property that makes
# the atomic model worth its cost -- while keeping the refusal shape for what is
# refused now, asserted as nothing reaching the engine.
# #169, second movement: FLOWABLE 280 -> 281, FULL_LOCAL 521 -> 522. The
# execution-evidence guard refused the manifest flip -- "Pool / Participant
# claims executes and has no row saying whether it was ever RUN" -- which is the
# guard doing exactly what #325 built it for. The row is a declaration
# (task-appears: the primary pool's user task appears when its process starts),
# and the live oracle gained the cell that measures it: ExecutionOracleSizeTests
# 50 -> 51, one more DeclaredEffects case. A participant has no activity row of
# its own, so it joins NeverEntered beside the data object reference.

# #170 (message flows execute): SLIM_BACKEND 2975 -> 2993, FLOWABLE 282 -> 286,
# FULL_LOCAL 523 -> 527. SLIM_E2E does not move.
#
# The eighteen slim facts are MessageFlowTests (fifteen: a drawn flow resolves to
# the target pool's key, message name and correlation key; an explicit attribute
# wins over the flow; the refusals -- dangling ref, not-a-sender, not-a-receiver,
# same pool, a flow drawn INTO a pool that deploys nothing -- each name the
# element), two EfCoreWorkflowModelStoreTests facts for the lookup by ANY key in
# a deployed set, and one WorkflowMessageCorrelatorTests fact for the
# counterpart link. The Message Flow oracle cell (one FLOWABLE fact, moved
# earlier in this story) is the manifest's `executes` claim measured on the
# engine; MessageFlowExecutionTests are the other four: a send from one pool
# starts the other and links the two instances BOTH ways, a receive task in the
# other pool is delivered to, an unrelated instance is NOT linked, and the empty-
# pool refusal leaves nothing in the engine.
#
# Identity: 240 + 286 + 4 - 3 = 527.

# #171 (a lane's group is the default assignment of its tasks): SLIM_BACKEND
# 2993 -> 3007, SLIM_E2E 240 -> 243, FLOWABLE 286 -> 290, FULL_LOCAL 527 -> 534.
#
# The fourteen slim backend facts are LaneAssignmentTests (twelve: the lane's
# group lands on the task with nothing of its own; a task's own assignee,
# candidate users or candidate groups are KEPT and the lane's group is NOT also
# written; a lane with no group and a task no lane lists get nothing; only user
# tasks are assigned; the innermost nested lane wins; the STORED diagram is not
# rewritten; a diagram without lanes is unchanged; extraction names the lane)
# and two FlowableClientTests facts (the actor's groups reach the engine as one
# comma-separated candidateGroups query, and an actor in no group issues none).
#
# The three slim E2E facts are LaneStudioTests, in the studio without an
# engine: a real mouse drag between lanes moves flowNodeRef membership -- the
# lane LEFT as well as the one entered -- not only the shape; a lane's group is
# picked from the groups that exist and comes back in the saved XML; a task in a
# lane says its assignment comes from the lane, naming the group.
#
# The four FLOWABLE facts are the Lane oracle cell (the lane deploys and the
# task it lists appears) and LaneAssignmentExecutionTests: a member of the
# lane's group sees the task and a NON-member does not; a task with its own
# assignee keeps it and the lane's group does NOT see it; a lane whose group was
# deleted is refused at publish, naming the lane, with nothing in the engine.
#
# Identity: 243 + 290 + 4 - 3 = 534.

